<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Migration Period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–600</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Barbarian Invasions, Völkerwanderungen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Migration Period, also known as the Barbarian Invasions, was a period of European history and late antiquity, from about 300 to 600, marked by large-scale migrations that saw the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> and the founding of post-Roman kingdoms on its former lands. It is traditionally dated from the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in about 375 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>' conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568. Among the peoples who moved were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, Alemanni, Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. Modern estimates suggest that between roughly 500,000 and over 1,000,000 people migrated into Roman territory in all.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When Roman authors first noticed them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> lived near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and to its north and east, pressing westward and southward towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In the late second century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> crushed some of the most powerful of them. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century">Crisis of the Third Century</a> new groupings with names such as Alemanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> formed near the Roman borders, while the Empire lost full control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, the regions east of the Middle Rhine and the Rhine delta.</p><p>Historians differ on the dates. The beginning is widely taken as the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> around 375 and the end as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568, though a looser period runs from as early as 300 to as late as 800. In 406 an armed group including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> made a particularly large crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The first wave, between 300 and 500, put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> in control of most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. The Tervingi crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 376, fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, and after a rebellion the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Odoacer deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> in 476, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> entered Gaul gradually, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a> in 486 they ruled northern Roman Gaul.</p><p>In the second wave, between 500 and 700, Slavic tribes spread through central, southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, making the eastern half of the continent predominantly Slavic-speaking, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> also moved. Group sizes varied from war bands of a few thousand to tribes of over 100,000. Modern estimates put the Gothic crossing of 376 at 90,000 to 200,000 people, and total migration into Roman territory at roughly 500,000 to over 1,000,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>German and Slavic scholars speak of migration, as in Völkerwanderung, while French and Italian historiography speaks of barbarian invasions. French and Italian scholars have tended to see a catastrophe and the start of a Dark Age, while German and English historians have often seen a decadent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> civilisation replaced by a more martial one.</p><p>Nineteenth-century scholars, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Johann Gottfried Herder</a>, treated tribes as coherent biological entities, which fed Pan-Germanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-slavism">Pan-Slavism</a>. Since the 1960s Goffart and Todd and others have argued that the Germani felt no shared identity, and the idea of ethnogenesis holds that tribes formed around a small nucleus of tradition. The Eastern Roman Empire adapted and survived until the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Migration Period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Migration Period</b>, from the 5th and 6th centuries, gold medallions called bracteates show a head above a horse-like beast, often read as Odin, and the picture stones of the island of Gotland depict scenes from myth.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
